]

Thought: The AlertEvidence entries confirm multiple serious alerts tied to vnevado-win10b and IP 141.216.110.153 (“Potential human-operated malicious activity”, “‘Kekeo’ malware…”, etc.), but they don’t include the AccountSid field (it’s blank in these rows). To get the SID for the account actually used during this suspicious activity, I should look at logon-related tables that capture account → SID on that device around the alert time, especially for users mentioned in the case description (kyleg, fk8mq) and on device vnevado-win10b.

The most appropriate table is likely DeviceLogonEvents (per-endpoint logons, with AccountName/SID). I will inspect its columns, then search for entries on vnevado-win10b around the time of the “Potential human-operated malicious activity” alert (around 2024-06-27 14:33–14:44) and filter for the accounts in question (kyleg, fk8mq). That should reveal the SID used in the suspicious activity.

Action: execute[
SHOW COLUMNS FROM DeviceLogonEvents;
]

